WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.110
Good morning, Hank; it's Friday.

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:04.009
I woke up early yesterday with nothing to
do but write and thought, "I think I might

00:00:04.009 --> 00:00:07.650
just go to Belgium today." So I walked down
to Amsterdam Central Station and bought a

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:13.070
ticket on the slow train to Antwerp. I wanted
time away from the Internet and my life, time

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:14.070
to write.

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:16.910
Hank, watching people write is boring, and
in my case at least it doesn't even involve

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:21.680
that much typing, but writing does, at least
for me, require quite a lot of concentration,

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:26.810
and to concentrate you must be prepared to
face the terrifying specter of boredom without

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:30.380
fear. In that respect, at least, both the
reading and writing of books have become kind

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:35.030
of countercultural activities in the social
media era. Books don't lend themselves to

00:00:35.030 --> 00:00:39.650
multitasking; they don't automatically update.
And as much as much as I love the Internet,

00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:41.390
I'm happiest when I'm writing.

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:44.320
Got into Antwerp knowing nothing about the
city, except that there are Winkles to the

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:48.250
left and Winkles to the right, and emerged
from the train station into this central square.

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:51.329
My general strategy for visiting a place I
haven't done any research about is just to

00:00:51.329 --> 00:00:55.290
find someone who looks like a tourist and
follow them to wherever they're going because

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:59.820
I figure they did the research for me. Then
I just stop at any old buildings and/or zoos.

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:03.290
This dude had a guidebook in his pocket so
I followed them, and sure enough, they led

00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:07.390
me to two sweet old buildings, although neither
was open. So I returned to the train station

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:11.540
to find more tourists to stalk whereupon I
noticed, "Holy crap there's the zoo."

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:16.200
At the zoo I saw little monkeys, and big monkeys,
and swingy monkeys, and this monkey with pants

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:20.190
picking up sticks for the presumed purpose
of building a nest, and this monkey with pants

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:25.229
planting ferns in some kind of primitive experiment
in agriculture, and almost puppy-sized elephants,

00:01:25.229 --> 00:01:29.710
and tigers, and zebra-giraffe-horses, and
this penguin that appeared to be decapitated

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:34.330
but was in fact just sleeping, and badass
fish, and a shark chatting with a seahorse

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:36.990
- okay, that was on television - and this
tortoise!

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:40.081
I tried to tell him that he needed to go on
and brush his shoulder off, on account of

00:01:40.081 --> 00:01:43.490
how his shoulder appeared to be storing the
fecal matter of a different tortoise, but

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:47.780
he either didn't understand me or he was making
a fashion statement. I also saw these giraffes

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:52.369
who were separated from me only by a ankle-high
fence and a tiny water fountain. Hank, I filmed

00:01:52.369 --> 00:01:55.950
them for like ten minutes, and they kept looking
at me like, "No matter how long you leave

00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:58.920
that camera on, we are not going to mount
each other."

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:02.521
Then I went back to the station where I rode
the worlds coolest escalator, up and then

00:02:02.521 --> 00:02:07.450
straight, and up again! How are these miraculous
escalators only in Belgium!? Did the Belgians

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:12.380
patent them? We must wrest this technology
from their tiny polylingual hands!

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:16.720
Minutes later at my train platform, I learned
that my train was "afgeschaft", and I was

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:18.590
like "I hope that doesn't mean delayed".

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:20.569
Since everyone is leaving, I think that was
bad.

00:02:20.569 --> 00:02:25.010
It means abolished. But another train came
an hour later, and I wrote all the way home.

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:28.530
And that, Hank, is how I lived for a day without
the Internet.

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:29.190
I'll see you on Monday.

